        { id: "s16", label: "Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map" },
        { id: "s17", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  

  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout info">CORRECTION FROM AN EARLIER POSTER: the §§290–293 poster flagged uncertainty about §295's content. Confirmed now: §295 is "(weggefallen)" – repealed. It formerly housed a consolidation PROHIBITION for subsidiaries with fundamentally dissimilar activities, based on the old EU Group Accounts Directive; this was abolished by the 2004 IFRS-Bilanzrechtsreformgesetz.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">From "who controls whom" to "who actually goes into the numbers"</div>
      <div class="principle">Where §290 established WHO must prepare a Konzernabschluss, and identified WHICH relationships constitute control, §§294–296 answer the NEXT question: of every subsidiary a parent controls, which ones must ACTUALLY be consolidated, and are any genuinely excludable?</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§294–296 MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Vollständigkeitsgebot, at group level</strong> – §294(1)'s default is INCLUSION of every controlled subsidiary, mirroring the same completeness presumption §246(1) established for individual assets and liabilities</span>
        <span><strong>Proportionality, narrowly applied</strong> – §296's exclusion grounds are genuinely NARROW exceptions to that default, not a broad opt-out</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §§294–296 require, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core requirement</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>294</span><span>Every subsidiary included by default; parent has a legal right to demand information</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>295</span><span>Repealed – formerly a dissimilar-activities exclusion, abolished in 2004</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>296</span><span>Four narrow exclusion grounds, with an anti-abuse aggregation rule</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Every subsidiary caught by the §290 control test</div>
      <div class="principle">§§294–296 apply to every subsidiary a parent has been determined to control under the §290(2) tests established in the previous poster – the DEFAULT rule is inclusion; exclusion is available ONLY through the specific, narrow §296 grounds.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§294(1) – Full Inclusion, Regardless of Registered Seat</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Foreign subsidiaries are captured just as fully as domestic ones</div>
      <div class="principle">§294(1) requires ALL subsidiaries of the parent to be included in the Konzernabschluss – REGARDLESS of their registered seat, meaning FOREIGN subsidiaries (wherever located) are captured exactly as fully as German ones, UNLESS a specific §296 exclusion applies. There is no general "foreign subsidiary" carve-out; only the narrow, named grounds in §296 (Section 7) can justify exclusion.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>§294(3) – the Parent's Legal Right to Information</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuine, legally enforceable right – not dependent on voluntary cooperation</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§294(3) grants the parent a genuine LEGAL RIGHT to demand from its subsidiaries – and from THEIR OWN subsidiaries, reaching down through multiple tiers – all documents and information necessary for preparing the Konzernabschluss. This ensures the parent's consolidation obligation is never practically frustrated by an uncooperative subsidiary's own management, since the parent can compel production rather than merely request it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Worked Example – Compelling a Reluctant Subsidiary to Cooperate</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A legal entitlement, reaching down through multiple tiers</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A sub-subsidiary's local management resisting disclosure</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A German parent's second-tier foreign sub-subsidiary – itself owned by a first-tier intermediate subsidiary – has local management reluctant to share detailed financial records, citing local confidentiality concerns.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">§294(3) gives the German parent a direct LEGAL RIGHT to demand the necessary information from this sub-subsidiary DESPITE it sitting two tiers down the ownership chain – local management's reluctance does not defeat this statutory right, and the parent may compel production through the intermediate subsidiary if necessary.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>§295 – Repealed, With a Genuinely Interesting History</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A former "dissimilar activities" prohibition, abolished in 2004</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§295 formerly implemented a consolidation PROHIBITION rooted in the old EU Group Accounts Directive's Article 14 – permitting (or in some readings, requiring) exclusion of a subsidiary whose activities were so DIFFERENT from the rest of the group that including it would be incompatible with giving a true and fair view (the classic example being a manufacturing group with one financial-services subsidiary). This provision was ABOLISHED by the 2004 IFRS-Bilanzrechtsreformgesetz, reflecting a broader international shift AWAY from dissimilar-activities exclusions – the SAME shift IFRS itself made when IAS 27/IFRS 10 eliminated their own equivalent exclusion, on the reasoning that dissimilar activities are better addressed through segment reporting than through excluding an entity from consolidation entirely.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>§296(1) – the Four Grounds for Excluding a Controlled Subsidiary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Narrow, specific, and optional – never automatic</div>
      <div class="principle">§296(1) permits (NEVER requires) a controlled subsidiary to be excluded from consolidation on any of the following grounds:</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Severe and lasting restrictions</strong> substantially impairing the parent's ability to exercise its rights over the subsidiary's assets or management (e.g. exchange controls, expropriation risk, war)</span>
        <span><strong>Disproportionate cost or delay</strong> in obtaining the information necessary for consolidation</span>
        <span><strong>Shares held exclusively with a view to subsequent resale</strong> (a genuinely temporary holding)</span>
        <span><strong>Subordinate importance</strong> (immateriality) to giving a true and fair view of the group's position</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example – Severe and Lasting Restrictions</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Political and currency risk genuinely preventing the exercise of control</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A subsidiary trapped by severe capital controls</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A German parent's subsidiary operates in a country that has imposed severe, sustained capital controls and expropriation risk following a political crisis, genuinely preventing the parent from repatriating funds, exercising voting control, or directing the subsidiary's management in any meaningful way.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The parent MAY exclude this subsidiary from consolidation under §296(1)'s first ground – the restrictions are both SEVERE and LASTING, substantially impairing the parent's actual ability to exercise its otherwise-existing control rights, DESPITE technical majority ownership remaining intact on paper.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example – Disproportionate Cost or Delay</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A genuinely high bar, not a convenience exemption</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: A recently-acquired subsidiary with incompatible legacy systems</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A parent acquires a small foreign subsidiary mid-year, whose legacy accounting systems are genuinely incompatible with the group's reporting requirements, such that obtaining consolidation-ready information within the reporting timeframe would require costs genuinely disproportionate to the subsidiary's own significance.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">PROVIDED the cost/delay is genuinely disproportionate – not merely inconvenient – the parent MAY rely on this ground for the current period, while working to integrate the subsidiary's systems for future consolidation. This ground is applied narrowly in practice; ordinary administrative friction from a new acquisition would generally NOT meet this bar on its own.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example – Shares Held Exclusively for Resale</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A genuinely temporary holding, illustrated by a bank rescue scenario</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: A bank's temporary rescue-driven shareholding</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A credit institution temporarily acquires a controlling shareholding in another troubled credit institution PURELY as part of a financial rescue/restructuring operation, with a clear, genuine intention to resell the shares once the rescued institution stabilises.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The acquiring bank MAY exclude the rescued institution from consolidation under §296(1)'s "held exclusively for resale" ground – a specifically recognised scenario in German banking-sector practice, though the bank must genuinely intend resale, not merely characterise an otherwise long-term holding as temporary to avoid consolidation.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example – Immateriality and the Aggregation Anti-Abuse Rule</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Several small exclusions cannot be used to hide a materially significant combined effect</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 5: Five individually immaterial subsidiaries</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A parent has five small subsidiaries, each individually immaterial to the group's overall picture, but whose COMBINED balance sheet totals and revenue would represent a materially significant portion of the group if considered together.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Even though EACH subsidiary individually qualifies for the immateriality exclusion ground, §296's aggregation rule REQUIRES all five to be included ANYWAY, since TOGETHER they are not of subordinate importance – the parent cannot use several small, individually-immaterial exclusions to achieve what would effectively be a materially significant omission from the group accounts.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>§296(3) – Justifying an Exclusion in the Konzernanhang</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Never a silent omission</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§296(3) requires ANY reliance on the §296(1)/(2) exclusion grounds to be JUSTIFIED and explained in the Konzernanhang (the consolidated notes, covered in a later poster) – a controlled subsidiary can never simply disappear from the group accounts without an explicit, disclosed reason readers can evaluate for themselves.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether restrictions are genuinely "severe and lasting," rather than temporary or manageable, under the first §296(1) ground</span>
            <span>Whether a stated resale intention under the third ground is genuine, versus a re-characterisation of an effectively long-term holding</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Testing the aggregation rule across a large population of small subsidiaries, particularly where individual immateriality assessments were made independently by different local teams</span>
            <span>Enforcing the §294(3) information right in practice against a genuinely uncooperative multi-tier sub-subsidiary in a jurisdiction with weak legal enforcement</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IFRS 10) – a Genuine, Significant Divergence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – HGB §296 vs IFRS 10</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB (§296)</span><span>IFRS 10</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Exclusion for severe restrictions</span><span>PERMITTED (§296(1))</span><span>NOT a general exclusion ground – IFRS 10 requires consolidation regardless in most such cases</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Exclusion for disproportionate cost</span><span>PERMITTED (§296(1))</span><span>NOT available – cost/effort is not a recognised IFRS 10 exclusion ground</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Held-for-resale exclusion</span><span>PERMITTED (§296(1))</span><span>Addressed narrowly through IFRS 5 (held-for-sale classification within consolidated figures), NOT full exclusion from consolidation itself</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Dissimilar activities exclusion</span><span>ABOLISHED in 2004 (former §295)</span><span>ABOLISHED – CONVERGED</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn">This is a genuinely significant, easily-missed divergence: while both frameworks abolished the OLD dissimilar-activities exclusion together, HGB's §296 STILL retains three considerably BROADER exclusion grounds (severe restrictions, disproportionate cost, held-for-resale) that IFRS 10's own, considerably STRICTER consolidation model generally does not offer nearly as liberally – a controlled subsidiary that HGB would permit a parent to exclude may well still require full IFRS 10 consolidation for a dual-reporting group.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§294–296 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Document(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Subsidiary included by default</span><span><span class="badge bo">Konzernabschluss</span></span><span>Full consolidation applies, per the later Vollkonsolidierung Titel</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>§296 exclusion relied upon</span><span><span class="badge be">Konzernanhang</span></span><span>Justification mandatory; subsidiary shown instead at cost/equity, per general principles</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §§294–296 connect across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§290(2)</span><span>Establishes the control tests that FIRST identify which enterprises are even candidates for §294 inclusion</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§300 et seq.</span><span>The Vollkonsolidierung (full consolidation) mechanics applied to every subsidiary this Titel determines must be included</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§313</span><span>The Konzernanhang provisions requiring the §296(3) exclusion justification to actually be delivered</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§340j (credit institutions)</span><span>Supplements §296(1)'s held-for-resale exclusion specifically for bank rescue scenarios</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Vorlage- und Auskunftspflicht</div><div class="glossary-def">Production and disclosure duty – the parent's legal right under §294(3) to demand consolidation-relevant information from subsidiaries.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Konsolidierungskreis</div><div class="glossary-def">Consolidation scope – the set of subsidiaries actually included in the Konzernabschluss.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Von untergeordneter Bedeutung</div><div class="glossary-def">Of subordinate importance – the immateriality ground for exclusion under §296(1), subject to the aggregation anti-abuse rule.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Vorübergehender Besitz</div><div class="glossary-def">Temporary holding – the basis for the held-for-resale exclusion ground, notably relevant to bank rescue scenarios.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Konzernanhang</div><div class="glossary-def">Consolidated notes, where any §296 exclusion must be justified per §296(3).</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Konzernbilanzrichtlinie</div><div class="glossary-def">The old EU Group Accounts Directive whose Article 14 underpinned the now-repealed §295 dissimilar-activities exclusion.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bo">Konzernabschluss</span> Consolidated financial statements
      <span class="badge be">Konzernanhang</span> Consolidated notes
    </div>
  </div>
  </div>
</StandardPageLayout>
